content.paragraph:
To verify this rule, let $B = \begin{bmatrix}
        \textbf{b}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{b}_{p}
        \end{bmatrix}$. Column $j$ of $A B$ is $A\textbf{b}_{j}$, and we can compute $A\textbf{b}_{j}$ by the row–vector rule for computing $A\textbf{x}$ from Section 1.4. The $i\,\text{t}\text{h}$ entry in $A\textbf{b}_{j}$ is the sum of the products of corresponding entries from row $i$ of $A$ and the vector $\textbf{b}_{j}$, which is precisely the computation described in the rule for computing the $(i,j)\text{-entry}$ of $A B$.﻿

