content.paragraph:
The formula in Theorem 5 is seldom used to solve an equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ numerically because row reduction of $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & \textbf{b}
        \end{bmatrix}$ is nearly always faster. (Row reduction is usually more accurate, too, when computations involve rounding off numbers.) One possible exception is the $2 \times 2$ case. In this case, mental computations to solve $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ are sometimes easier using the formula for $A^{- 1}$, as in the next example.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
        {E_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 1 & 0 \\
        {- 4} & 0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix},\quad E_{2} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 1 & 0 \\
        1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 1
        \end{bmatrix},\quad E_{3} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 0 & 0 \\
        0 & 1 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 & 5
        \end{bmatrix},} \\
        {A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        a & b & c \\
        d & e & f \\
        g & h & i
        \end{bmatrix}}
        \end{matrix}$$

$$\begin{matrix}
        {E_{1}A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        a & b & c \\
        d & e & f \\
        {g - 4a} & {h - 4b} & {i - 4c}
        \end{bmatrix},\quad E_{2}A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        d & e & f \\
        a & b & c \\
        g & h & i
        \end{bmatrix},} \\
        {E_{3}A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
        a & b & c \\
        d & e & f \\
        {5g} & {5h} & {5i}
        \end{bmatrix}\text{.}}
        \end{matrix}$$

content.paragraph:
If we place $A$ and $I$ side by side to form an augmented matrix $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & I
        \end{bmatrix}$, then row operations on this matrix produce identical operations on $A$ and on $I$. By Theorem 7, either there are row operations that transform $A$ to $I_{n}$ and $I_{n}$ to $A^{- 1}$ or else $A$ is not invertible.

note.paragraph:
Row reduce the augmented matrix $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & I
        \end{bmatrix}$. If $A$ is row equivalent to $I$, then $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & I
        \end{bmatrix}$ is row equivalent to $\begin{bmatrix}
        I & A^{- 1}
        \end{bmatrix}$. Otherwise, $A$ does not have an inverse.

content.paragraph:
Denote the columns of $I_{n}$ by $\textbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\textbf{e}_{n}$. Then row reduction of $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & I
        \end{bmatrix}$ to $\begin{bmatrix}
        I & A^{- 1}
        \end{bmatrix}$ can be viewed as the simultaneous solution of the $n$ systems

where the “augmented columns” of these systems have all been placed next to $A$ to form $\begin{bmatrix}
        A & \textbf{e}_{1} & \textbf{e}_{2} & \cdots & \textbf{e}_{n}
        \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
        A & I
        \end{bmatrix}$. The equation $AA^{- 1} = I$ and the definition of matrix multiplication show that the columns of $A^{- 1}$ are precisely the solutions of the systems in (2). This observation is useful because some applied problems may require finding only one or two columns of $A^{- 1}$. In this case, only the corresponding systems in (2) need to be solved.

