        \end{array} \right\rbrack\,\mspace{-4mu}\mspace{-4mu},$ and $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}\text{.}$ Use the figure to estimate ${\lbrack\textbf{x}\rbrack_{B}},\lbrack\textbf{y}\rbrack_{B}\text{,}$ and $\lbrack\textbf{z}\rbrack_{B}\text{.}$ Confirm your estimates of $\lbrack\textbf{y}\rbrack_{B}$ and $\lbrack\textbf{z}\rbrack_{B}$ by using them and $\{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}$ to compute $\mathbf{y}$ and $\mathbf{z}$.

Let $A = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 0 \\
      0 & {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\text{,}$ $B = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      0 & 1 \\
      1 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$. These are Pauli spin matrices used in the study of electron spin in quantum mechanics. Show that $A^{2} = I\text{,}$ $B^{2} = I\text{,}$ and $A B = - B\mspace{-180mu} A$. Matrices such that $A B = - B\mspace{-180mu} A$ are said to anticommute.

Find a matrix $A$ such that the transformation $\textbf{x}\mapsto\! A\textbf{x}$ maps $\begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      3
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      7
      \end{bmatrix}$ into $\begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix},$respectively. [Hint:Write a matrix equation involving $A\text{,}$ and solve for $A$.]

